      "text": "\\begin{align*}\n  \\operatorname{wait} & = 4 \\cdot 8^2 + 7 \\cdot 8^1 + 3\\cdot 8^0\\\\\n                    & = 4\\cdot 64 + 7\\cdot 8 + 3\\\\\n                    & = 256 + 56 + 3\\\\\n                    & = \\boxed{315}\n\\end{align*}. \nOur general solution must be:\n$$ \\begin{align*} l & = 4\\times 8^3+7\\times 8^2+3\\times 8^1+3\\cdot 8^0\\\\  & = 2048+320+24+3\\\\  & = 2395\\end{align*} $$\nBut this is formulaic.  Now you guys challenge each and every one of you, and we will see who can be faster.\nIt is a lengthy calculation since it involves big numbers but the Maple CAS finds it in less than a millisecond.\n\nIf you dispose with resorting to Maple however, here is an alternate method to arrive to the same answer. The bit length of $473_{10}$ in base 8 can be enumerated by repeatedly taking its value to 32, and each cycle of extracting its top bit can be done like so:\n$473_{10}\\div8 = 59 \\ $\\ $\\div 8 = 7 \\ $\\ $\\div 8 = $\\bf{1}$\n$\\therefore \\ $ $32\\cdot\\lceil(59/8^2)\\rceil + 2\\cdot\\lceil(59/8)\\rceil + 2 = 32 + 2\\cdot2 + 4 + 3 = 41$\nNow, total number of digits = $41-3 = 38$, subtract $38-3=35$\n$8^3 = 512$\n\nRemark: For an $a\\cdot8^b+c$, it is best to realize that each cycle of extracting top bits contributes $8^b - 8^{b-2}$ thus $S:=a\\cdot8^b=\\sum_i 8^{3i} + 8^{2i} = 512 + 128 + 32 + 4 + 0$, and thus the answer is $\\boxed 36$.\n\n[color=#fff]EDITTED: simplified beforehand expression of $l$.\n\n(NOTE: Higher degree terms can be ignored due to cut off statement)\nThe number of bits is equal to $l=\\lfloor{473/8}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{(473/8^2) + (473/8^3)}\\rfloor+\\cdots = 35 + \\lfloor{35/8^4}\\rfloor  = 35+7 = 42$ Although  another method can be founded, I posted this for the posterity of the thread. Thanks. :rotfl: The sum of its digits should be $36$, while the method above is right ;)\n\nThe sum of digits is 36. I can hold my comparison usage if you can find a 'detailed process' then repet wrongly...thanks  :maybe: Is that 'detailed process' from your head or a process you made the teacher show to you? Or is the method you made yourself?\n\nI cannot cite it, because you are generalizing your snoring waking arab.\nA question.\n\nCan someone explain to me why  the powers  of 8, 8^0,8^1,8^2,8^3... (used to 473 / 8 in each step) have a  decreasing values from 8 ^0 to 8 ^ 2?\n\nand why the numberof next value in the next step till  8^3 is 7  , while its not 8^1,8^0 ...?\nNothing prevents me from making a ten minute video here and pasting it here.  No reason to make a new thread on every elementary problem.\n\nThis is because if the latter number were to be fractional, i.e. not terminating, the answer would be 0.",
